  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Added:\n- Turnstile options are now validated when the host starts. A missing or empty\n  secret key fails the application with a message naming the property, instead\n  of leaving every verification to fail silently against Cloudflare at request\n  time. Note that the secret key is required whatever the Enabled flag says: an\n  application that ran with verification switched off and no secret configured\n  will not start until one is set. Cloudflare's testing keys, such as\n  1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA, are made for exactly that, and the error\n  message says so.\n- The verification HttpClient is now registered with a 10-second timeout in\n  place of the framework default of 100 seconds, so an unresponsive endpoint no\n  longer holds a request thread. The overload that exposes the HttpClient\n  builder still overrides it, resilience handlers included.\n\nChanged:\n- MVC actions now skip verification for GET, HEAD, and OPTIONS requests, as\n  Razor Pages handlers already did. Applying the validation attribute to a whole\n  controller no longer demands a Turnstile token from every read it serves.\n  Check your annotations before upgrading: an attribute placed deliberately on a\n  GET action stops verifying, silently and without a model-state error. Move it\n  to the action that handles the post.\n- The site key is no longer required. It belongs to the browser widget and the\n  server-side verification path never reads it, so applications that render the\n  widget elsewhere, or render none at all, no longer have to configure a value.\n  The secret key stays required.\n\nFixed:\n- A siteverify response that cannot be read as Cloudflare's documented JSON --\n  an empty or malformed body, or an error page served by a proxy under a 200 --\n  now resolves to a failed verification instead of surfacing as an unhandled\n  server error on the protected form post. Caller-initiated cancellation still\n  propagates.",
